# A pine, for the battle backdrop and nothing else.
#
# Y is up, +Z is north, one world unit is one map tile. The lowest vertex sits
# on y = 0 and the model is centred on x = 0 and z = 0, so the game places it
# by its feet.
#
# Faces are wound so that (c - a) x (b - a) points out of the solid, which is
# what tools/obj2cpp.py bakes as the face normal. Get it backwards and the
# model still draws, it just goes flat ambient dark. The ring convention here
# is rock.obj's: (r cos t, y, r sin t) with the angle increasing, cones wound
# apex then ring[i] then ring[i+1], and the trunk's quads wound top[i],
# bottom[i], bottom[i+1], top[i+1].
#
# Three overlapping six sided cones and a four sided trunk: 26 triangles,
# 2.05 units tall.
#
# The overworld does NOT use this. There a tree is a sprite, because a
# screenful of Route 1 is up to 87 of them and 26 triangles each is more
# triangles than the rest of the frame put together. The battle backdrop has
# five, they are the only scenery in the shot, and geometry there catches the
# light and sits in the depth buffer in a way a billboard cannot.

mtllib pine.mtl

v 0.000 2.050 0.000
v 0.340 1.320 0.000
v 0.170 1.320 0.294
v -0.170 1.320 0.294
v -0.340 1.320 0.000
v -0.170 1.320 -0.294
v 0.170 1.320 -0.294
v 0.000 1.620 0.000
v 0.500 0.920 0.000
v 0.250 0.920 0.433
v -0.250 0.920 0.433
v -0.500 0.920 0.000
v -0.250 0.920 -0.433
v 0.250 0.920 -0.433
v 0.000 1.200 0.000
v 0.620 0.520 0.000
v 0.310 0.520 0.537
v -0.310 0.520 0.537
v -0.620 0.520 0.000
v -0.310 0.520 -0.537
v 0.310 0.520 -0.537
v 0.100 0.620 0.000
v 0.000 0.620 0.100
v -0.100 0.620 0.000
v -0.000 0.620 -0.100
v 0.130 0.000 0.000
v 0.000 0.000 0.130
v -0.130 0.000 0.000
v -0.000 0.000 -0.130

usemtl pine_needles
f 1 2 3
f 1 3 4
f 1 4 5
f 1 5 6
f 1 6 7
f 1 7 2
f 8 9 10
f 8 10 11
f 8 11 12
f 8 12 13
f 8 13 14
f 8 14 9
f 15 16 17
